      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "This study was undertaken to evaluate the ability of predischarge low-dose dobutamine echocardiography to predict late left ventricular functional recovery after thrombolyzed acute myocardial infarction."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "METHODS AND RESULTS",
          "content": "Low-dose dobutamine echocardiography was performed in 54 patients 4 +/- 2 days after acute myocardial infarction treated with thrombolysis. Follow-up resting echocardiography was carried out in 49 of these patients at a mean of 18 +/- 6 months later. Viability was defined as recovery of myocardial function at follow-up, expressed as an improvement of wall motion of at least 1 grade or more in at least 2 contiguous infarct zone segments. In 24 of the 49 patients (group I), wall motion at follow-up improved in comparison with the early resting echocardiographic study (1.72 +/- 0.29 vs 1.37 +/- 0.34, P <.001). In the remaining 25 patients (group II), no wall motion enhancement was seen at follow-up (1.57 +/- 0.38 vs 1.58 +/- 0.36, NS). In 22 of the 24 patients in group I, early low-dose dobutamine echocardiography showed improvement in the wall motion score index compared with baseline resting measurements (1.72 +/- 0.29 vs 1.44 +/- 0.24, P <.001). The positive and negative predictive value of early low-dose dobutamine echocardiography to predict functional recovery was 76% and 92%, respectively."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Predischarge low-dose dobutamine echocardiography is an accurate tool for detecting viable myocardium and predicting late left ventricular recovery after acute myocardial infarction treated with thrombolysis."
        }
      ]},
